<h1>Running Hello World test</h1>
<p>With the test's code reviewed, let's run it. As code developers, we'll start from the CLI, then revisit the
  same run through the controller's GUI, which the <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/">Getting
  started</a> guide already walked through click by click.</p>

<h2 id="running-from-cli">Running from CLI</h2>
<p>The test's <code>main</code> function makes it <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/test/#standalone">directly runnable
  as a script</a> - from the test's own directory:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("cd orcraft-test-suite-tutorial/test_group_intro/test_hello_world") }}
{{ cmd.copyCommand("python test_hello_world.py") }}
<h3 id="ansi-output">ANSI output</h3>
<p>The framework provides structured, formatted output for each stage of the test, from configuration to results:</p>
{% ansiOutput "hello-world-run.ansi" %}
<p>Notice the <code>Hello World</code> name and description in the output - these are exactly the <code>name</code>
  and <code>description</code> fields set on
  <a class="text-accent" href="/tutorial/hello-world-test-structure/#config-class">TestHelloWorldConfig</a>
  earlier. The rest of the output -
  timing, the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/configuration-fields/">configuration table</a>, the
  <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/statuses/#statuses">pass/fail result</a> - is the framework at work: the test itself only
  logged one line, and the framework did everything else. Each of these built-in pieces is covered in its own
  place, later in this tutorial and in the <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/">Framework</a> section.</p>
<h3 id="log-files">Log files</h3>
<p>This same output is also <a class="text-accent" href="/framework/logging/#log-files">saved alongside the test</a>, in a
  <code>Logs</code> folder created next to it:</p>
{{ cmd.copyCommand("cd Logs") }}
{{ cmd.copyCommand("ls") }}
{% ansiOutput "hello-world-logs-dir.txt" %}

<h2 id="running-from-gui">Running from GUI</h2>
<p>Running this test from the controller's GUI - adding the suite, selecting the test and starting the run - is
  already covered step by step in the <a class="text-accent" href="/setup/getting-started/">Getting started</a>
  guide.</p>

<h3 id="test-log">Test log</h3>
